 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: AI free fall 
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep ; Chart: Pete Gannon/Axios Tech stocks plunged today, reflecting investors' growing fears of a slowdown in the AI boom.

 📉 The latest: The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite index closed down 5.5%, firmly in correction territory, down almost 12% from its high on Oct. 6.

 The S&amp;P 500 wasn't far behind today, shedding 4.4%. The sell-off is the stock market's worst showing since the April 2025 tariff panic . 🤖 The big picture: It comes two days after Democrats notched historic gains in the midterm elections, including taking the governorships of Texas and Ohio — areas of intense data center development.

 In Texas, Gov.-elect Gina Hinojosa promised to impose a moratorium on new buildouts until lawmakers enact stronger protections. In Ohio, Amy Acton called for conditional pauses requiring developers to cover their utility costs, provide community benefits and use union labor. Between the lines: Their victories signal growing political resistance to arguably the largest investment boom in U.S. history.

 🔍 Zoom in: AI-related shares led today's sell-off, with companies closely tied to the data center build-out posting double-digit losses.

 Semiconductor shares plummeted, dragging the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index down over 12%. The index is now down more than 38% from its June high. So-called neoclouds — companies that build data centers in order to lease out their computing power to larger players like hyperscalers — were particularly hard hit. CoreWeave, for example, was down 9.8%. Even the Big Tech hyperscalers that supported the indexes with gains a day ago — Microsoft (-3.6%), Alphabet (-1.9%) and Amazon (-2.2%) — were caught in today's market downdraft. 
 
